+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others — SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys
+- Status: superseded on 2026-10-23. The maintainer explicitly overrode this decision; §contiguous-tab-keys below
+  replaces it. The original record is kept verbatim below for history and is no longer in force. The "close keeps
+  keys" test it cites was replaced the same day by the contiguous-key tests.
+
+> - Since: 2026-09-02
+> - Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
+> - Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
+> - Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
+>   and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
+> - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others so keys stay contiguous
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `closeTab` removes the tab, then rekeys the remaining tabs in their existing order as `t1, t2, …, tn` and
+  sets `next` to n+1, so the next `openTab` gets `t(n+1)` and never collides. Keys are always contiguous; a key names
+  a position in the strip, not a fixed tab.
+- Forbidden: leaving gaps in the keys after a close (`t1,t3`).
+- Overrides: §stable-tab-keys. The maintainer made this decision on 2026-10-23, explicitly and knowingly overriding it.
+- Accepted cost: the hazard recorded in §stable-tab-keys still applies. Every tab to the right of a closed tab gets a
+  new key, so as a React key it remounts (scroll position and unsaved form input are lost), and a `?tabs=` URL or any
+  key held from before a close may now name a different tab. Callers must re-read keys from state after a close.
 - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
